1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,458
Song Ong Siang: The Chronicler Who

2
00:00:02,538 --> 00:00:05,450
Became Singapore's First Chinese Knight

3
00:00:05,450 --> 00:00:09,929
If you have read much history
of the Singapore Chinese,

4
00:00:10,009 --> 00:00:13,741
you have almost certainly
relied on a 602-page

5
00:00:13,821 --> 00:00:17,800
book published in London
in 1923, at £12 a copy.

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:22,157
Its author, Song Ong Siang, was
a lawyer, a magazine editor,

7
00:00:22,237 --> 00:00:25,411
a volunteer captain and
eventually a knight,

8
00:00:25,491 --> 00:00:28,375
and yet few people today know his name.

9
00:00:28,375 --> 00:00:32,727
He was born in Singapore on
the 14th of June 1871 into

10
00:00:32,807 --> 00:00:36,256
a Straits Chinese
family, the locally born,

11
00:00:36,336 --> 00:00:41,425
English-educated Chinese community
also called the Peranakans.

12
00:00:41,425 --> 00:00:45,720
His father, Song Hoot Kiam,
was a Presbyterian who

13
00:00:45,800 --> 00:00:50,175
founded the Chinese Christian
Association in 1889.

14
00:00:50,175 --> 00:00:54,853
Song went to Raffles Institution
from 1878, won the Guthrie

15
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:59,450
Scholarship five times, and
was head prefect at fifteen.

16
00:00:59,450 --> 00:01:04,419
The great prize for a bright colonial
schoolboy was the Queen's Scholarship,

17
00:01:04,499 --> 00:01:06,625
which paid for study in Britain.

18
00:01:06,625 --> 00:01:11,876
Song came first in the examination
in both 1886 and 1887 and

19
00:01:11,956 --> 00:01:17,375
was passed over each time because
he was under the age limit.

20
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:20,685
Lim Boon Keng, later his collaborator,

21
00:01:20,765 --> 00:01:25,850
won in 1887 and became the
first Chinese Queen's Scholar.

22
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:29,964
Song finally took the
scholarship in 1888, and he was

23
00:01:30,044 --> 00:01:34,475
the only Chinese Queen's
Scholar to read law in England.

24
00:01:34,475 --> 00:01:39,874
He entered the Middle Temple in
1888 and went up to Downing College,

25
00:01:39,954 --> 00:01:44,225
Cambridge, in 1890, taking
a second-class law degree.

26
00:01:44,225 --> 00:01:47,939
He was called to the
English Bar in June 1893,

27
00:01:48,019 --> 00:01:53,466
and on the 20th of March 1894 he
was admitted to the Singapore Bar,

28
00:01:53,546 --> 00:01:56,350
the first Chinese barrister there.

29
00:01:56,350 --> 00:02:00,499
With his Cambridge classmate
James Aitken he founded

30
00:02:00,579 --> 00:02:04,322
the firm Aitken & Ong
Siang, and after Aitken's

31
00:02:04,402 --> 00:02:08,550
death in 1928 he became
the doyen of the local Bar.

32
00:02:08,550 --> 00:02:12,603
A tribute in the Straits
Times in 1936 described him as

33
00:02:12,683 --> 00:02:17,038
a lawyer who preferred quiet
chamber work to the courtroom,

34
00:02:17,118 --> 00:02:20,725
whose desk was "usually
piled high with papers."

35
00:02:20,725 --> 00:02:24,775
Song's public life ran
alongside his practice.

36
00:02:24,775 --> 00:02:28,591
In 1894 he started a short-lived newspaper

37
00:02:28,671 --> 00:02:31,825
in Romanised Malay, Bintang Timor.

38
00:02:31,825 --> 00:02:37,428
In 1897 he and Lim Boon Keng launched
the Straits Chinese Magazine,

39
00:02:37,508 --> 00:02:39,888
an English-language quarterly

40
00:02:39,968 --> 00:02:44,125
that ran until 1907 and
folded for lack of funds.

41
00:02:44,125 --> 00:02:49,359
In 1899 the two men helped found
the Singapore Chinese Girls' School,

42
00:02:49,439 --> 00:02:54,212
and in 1900 Song was secretary
when the Straits Chinese British

43
00:02:54,292 --> 00:02:59,375
Association was formed with Lim,
Tan Jiak Kim and Seah Liang Seah.

44
00:02:59,375 --> 00:03:02,275
He was also a soldier of sorts.

45
00:03:02,275 --> 00:03:06,556
When the Chinese company of the
Singapore Volunteer Infantry

46
00:03:06,636 --> 00:03:10,482
was formed in 1901, he was
among its original members,

47
00:03:10,562 --> 00:03:15,206
and in 1902 he was one of seven
men chosen to represent it in the

48
00:03:15,286 --> 00:03:19,575
Straits contingent at King
Edward the Seventh's coronation.

49
00:03:19,575 --> 00:03:23,400
He was promoted captain in 1915.

50
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:29,274
He served on the Legislative Council
as an unofficial member from 1921,

51
00:03:29,354 --> 00:03:33,132
with a break, until 1927,
and he sat on a 1926

52
00:03:33,212 --> 00:03:37,409
committee on Chinese marriage
that backed monogamy,

53
00:03:37,489 --> 00:03:43,275
a reform that led eventually to the
Civil Marriage Ordinance of 1941.

54
00:03:43,275 --> 00:03:45,625
The book began small.

55
00:03:45,625 --> 00:03:49,987
Singapore's 1919 centenary called
for a commemorative volume,

56
00:03:50,067 --> 00:03:52,244
One Hundred Years of Singapore,

57
00:03:52,324 --> 00:03:56,977
and Song was asked to write two
or three chapters on the Chinese,

58
00:03:57,057 --> 00:04:00,625
after Lim Boon Keng declined
and recommended him.

59
00:04:00,625 --> 00:04:04,850
The chapters grew into a
full chronological history.

60
00:04:04,850 --> 00:04:09,840
Working with two assistants and members
of the Straits Chinese Reading Club,

61
00:04:09,920 --> 00:04:13,509
he spent about three years
going through old newspapers

62
00:04:13,589 --> 00:04:17,725
and asking prominent families
for sketches of their forebears.

63
00:04:17,725 --> 00:04:19,832
It was finished in 1922,

64
00:04:19,912 --> 00:04:23,375
but the cost of printing was daunting.

65
00:04:23,375 --> 00:04:27,594
Song arranged for John Murray
to publish it in London,

66
00:04:27,674 --> 00:04:31,335
and it appeared in November
1923 at £12 a copy,

67
00:04:31,415 --> 00:04:35,475
more than a month's wages
for most Chinese workers.

68
00:04:35,475 --> 00:04:39,151
Subscriptions were poor
until an open letter from the

69
00:04:39,231 --> 00:04:43,625
Reverend William Murray in the
Straits Times helped rescue it.

70
00:04:43,625 --> 00:04:46,433
The dedication, which
the paper later quoted,

71
00:04:46,513 --> 00:04:50,797
thanked his late father and his
wife, whose "encouragement and help"

72
00:04:50,877 --> 00:04:54,600
had sustained him through
"three years of incessant toil."

73
00:04:54,600 --> 00:04:59,450
The Times Literary Supplement
reviewed it enthusiastically.

74
00:04:59,450 --> 00:05:03,095
What made it valuable was
its portraits and biographies

75
00:05:03,175 --> 00:05:06,616
of the merchants, planters
and community leaders who

76
00:05:06,696 --> 00:05:10,150
built the colony, many of
whom appear nowhere else.

77
00:05:10,150 --> 00:05:14,499
This blog has leaned on it for
its portrait of pineapple magnate

78
00:05:14,579 --> 00:05:18,800
Lim Nee Soon in the pineapple
kings post, among other things.

79
00:05:18,800 --> 00:05:22,294
He was knighted in the
New Year Honours of 1936,

80
00:05:22,374 --> 00:05:25,495
and the Straits Times
reported it under the

81
00:05:25,575 --> 00:05:29,000
sub-head "First Chinese
Knighthood In Malaya."

82
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:33,020
A week later the paper ran a
tribute by a Penang Straits Chinese

83
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:36,735
writer signing himself "L.C.L.",
who called him "The First

84
00:05:36,815 --> 00:05:40,322
Straits-Born Chinese Knight"
and praised his gentleness,

85
00:05:40,402 --> 00:05:44,950
modesty and "very highest sense of
honour in the small things of life."

86
00:05:44,950 --> 00:05:48,740
Song was proud of a description
of his community as "the

87
00:05:48,820 --> 00:05:52,886
King's Chinese," and the paper
later quoted a tribute to his

88
00:05:52,966 --> 00:05:57,250
"simultaneous loyalty" to his
own community and to the Throne.

89
00:05:57,250 --> 00:06:01,900
Ill health withdrew him from
public life in his last years.

90
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:06,931
He died on the 29th of September
1941, and the next day the Straits

91
00:06:07,011 --> 00:06:11,966
Times published an editorial headed
"A Great Gentleman," declaring

92
00:06:12,046 --> 00:06:16,775
that no citizen, Asian or European,
was more worthy of honour.

93
00:06:16,775 --> 00:06:21,145
His funeral was held with
military honours, and his coffin,

94
00:06:21,225 --> 00:06:25,750
under a Union Jack, was drawn
on a gun carriage to Bidadari.

95
00:06:25,750 --> 00:06:29,194
His firm was dissolved at the end of 1941,

96
00:06:29,274 --> 00:06:34,225
when the Japanese invasion of
Malaya was already under way.

97
00:06:34,225 --> 00:06:37,325
Scholars have judged the book differently.

98
00:06:37,325 --> 00:06:41,481
Paul Wheatley, writing in 1969,
thought it concentrated on

99
00:06:41,561 --> 00:06:45,717
the English-educated Straits
Chinese elite and said almost

100
00:06:45,797 --> 00:06:50,325
nothing about the labourers who
made up most of the community.

101
00:06:50,325 --> 00:06:54,148
James Warren, who wrote on
the lives of poorer Chinese,

102
00:06:54,228 --> 00:06:59,550
argued that it records more about
ordinary Chinese than most earlier works.

103
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:02,660
Both views describe the
same sprawling book,

104
00:07:02,740 --> 00:07:06,075
and historians of the
period still turn to it.

105
00:07:06,075 --> 00:07:09,151
I found no single explanation for why its

106
00:07:09,231 --> 00:07:12,925
author is remembered so
much less than his book.

107
00:07:12,925 --> 00:07:17,198
What the record does show is a
man who spent a lifetime building

108
00:07:17,278 --> 00:07:22,300
institutions for his own community,
wrote its history when nobody else had,

109
00:07:22,380 --> 00:07:26,325
and was mourned by people of
every community when he died.

110
00:07:26,325 --> 00:07:30,233
Why it matters today: Much
of what Singapore knows about

111
00:07:30,313 --> 00:07:34,078
its nineteenth-century Chinese
pioneers comes from one

112
00:07:34,158 --> 00:07:38,075
book written by a man most
Singaporeans could not name.

113
00:07:38,075 --> 00:07:41,374
Song Ong Siang is a
reminder that the history we

114
00:07:41,454 --> 00:07:44,330
inherit was compiled by particular people,

115
00:07:44,410 --> 00:07:48,835
with particular loyalties and
blind spots, and that they deserve

116
00:07:48,915 --> 00:07:52,575
to be read as carefully as
the events they recorded.
